Repairs: "Keep verb dependencies compatible with predecessor installers", "Plan and install declared product prerequisites" and "Reuse revision-isolated Cargo installation caches and verified dependencies" — the defect that installing one product rebuilt each dependency to its head and refused a consumer whose provider the machine does not have, so products that depend on each other could not be installed and none ran without the others.
Harsh-Review-Attacked: reusing an old dependency could leave a consumer without a capability it calls - the consumer's needs are its contract's verbs, checked by the prerequisite decision against the installed provider's own manifest, and an installed provider without the verb is still refused.
Harsh-Review-Attacked: a broken or tampered dependency could now be reused - reuse still requires a completed receipt, the same recipe and destination, recorded files and a passing ownership and signature verification.
Harsh-Review-Attacked: skipping an absent provider could hide that the consumer cannot work - the consumer's own call of the verb reports the provider's absence at run time, as Stado's admission already says no Oko is installed.
Harsh-Review-Attacked: the reuse report could name a revision that was not installed - it now reports the receipt's own source revision instead of the checkout head.
Harsh-Review-Attacked: the real prerequisite journey could keep asserting the refusal of --without - it now asserts that the plan without the provider, and the plan with --without, name no prerequisite step, beside the unchanged refusal for an installed older provider.
